anation":"This puzzle is built from a fixed grid of compartments, and each compartment is resolved on its own.\n\n**Structure.** Every grid is made of 9×9 compartments placed at a period of 10. The sizes 9, 19 and 29 all fit this. Thick lines in colours 5, 7 or 9 separate the compartments. Inside each compartment there are four 3×3 blocks, with top-left corners at offsets (1,1), (1,5), (5,1) and (5,5).\n\nA compartment may also contain a thin colour-4 line through its middle. That line is either row 4 or column 4 of the compartment, and it runs along the whole compartment. In pair 2 that row runs across the entire grid, so I don't use the generic divider detection. I read compartments by their fixed positions instead.\n\n**What happens in each compartment (checked against every pair):**\n- **Vertical colour-4 line at column 4.** The left and right blocks swap. In pair 1, `2|1 / 1|8` becomes `1|2 / 8|1`.\n- **Horizontal colour-4 line at row 4.** The top and bottom block rows swap. In pair 2's lower compartment, `3 9 / 2 8` becomes `2 8 / 3 9`. Pair 4's lower-left compartment behaves the same way.\n- **No line.** Three of the blocks share one colour (1 in every case shown) and one block has a different colour. All four blocks take that odd colour. Examples: 6 in pair 2, 8 in pair 3, 4 and 2 in pair 4. I pick the least common block colour, so the rule does not depend on the filler being 1.\n\n**On the tests.**\n- Test 1 has three no-line compartments, which become 6, 4 and 8. It has two line compartments. The top-left one has a vertical line and swaps left/right. The bottom-right one has a horizontal line and swaps top/bottom.\n- Test 2 has nine compartments, and each gets the same treatment. For example, the top-right compartment `1 7 / 1 1` becomes all 7. The bottom-left compartment has a vertical line, so its left and right blocks swap.\n\n**Teaching stages.** I emit the canvas once after each compartment is resolved, and then emit the final grid.\n\n\n\n(`Counter` comes from the `collections` module, which is allowed; if it is not pre-imported, add `from collections import Counter` at the top of the function.)","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    offs = [(1, 1), (1, 5), (5, 1), (5, 5)]\n    for cy in range(0, H - 8, 10):\n        for cx in range(0, W - 8, 10):\n            def blk(i):\n                oy, ox = offs[i]\n                return g[cy+oy:cy+oy+3, cx+ox:cx+ox+3].copy()\n            def put(i, b):\n                oy, ox = offs[i]\n                out[cy+oy:cy+oy+3, cx+ox:cx+ox+3] = b\n            col = g[cy:cy+9, cx+4]\n            row = g[cy+4, cx:cx+9]\n            if np.all(col != bg) and len(set(col.tolist())) == 1:\n                b = [blk(i) for i in range(4)]\n                put(0, b[1]); put(1, b[0]); put(2, b[3]); put(3, b[2])\n                emit(out, f\"Compartment at ({cy},{cx}) has a vertical divider: swap left and right blocks.\")\n            elif np.all(row != bg) and len(set(row.tolist())) == 1:\n                b = [blk(i) for i in range(4)]\n                put(0, b[2]); put(2, b[0]); put(1, b[3]); put(3, b[1])\n                emit(out, f\"Compartment at ({cy},{cx}) has a horizontal divider: swap top and bottom blocks.\")\n            else:\n                cols = [int(blk(i)[0, 0]) for i in range(4)]\n                cnt = Counter(cols)\n                odd = min(cnt, key=lambda c: cnt[c])\n                for i in range(4):\n                    put(i, np.full((3, 3), odd))\n                emit(out, f\"Compartment at ({cy},{cx}) has no divider: fill all blocks with the odd colour {odd}.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 2 that row runs across the entire grid, so I don't use the generic divider detection."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Compartment at (0,0) has a vertical divider: swap left and right blocks."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Compartment at (10,0) has a horizontal divider: swap top and bottom blocks."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Structure.** Every grid is made of 9×9 compartments placed at a period of 10."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"435e694dc4633c7c84f996082772b517c607fa4c75a69dece04a7cd694860d97","task_sha256":"8449525c3b7b373042c9d9d34b9f1b5747c98bf599907b41ca4c5ed3a505f182","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
